{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1430015789,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI5MDEyOTg1NA=="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1430340538,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk3NTgxNTcw","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-97581570"},"message":"A generic stdin interface would need some means to indicate when a\ncommand had finished running (and probably its exit status). This would\nseem to need some kind of serialization around commands and since they\ncan output anything to stdout, I don't see a good way to do that.\n\nIt would be simple to add --batch to particular plumbing commands\nthough. This seems to be more the way to go to me, I imagine you\nprobably have 2 or 3 plumbing commands that are the bulk of the\noverhead.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1430343113,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk3NTkyMDcw","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-97592070"},"message":"yeah -- serialization probably would be quite a bit more of work... but what about some socket, e.g unix domain sockets on linux/osx and regular tcp/ip one on windows -- this way all commands/status could be reported through it and we swallow stdout/err from the annex invocation to get them if/when we care?  if you/tools flush it upon command completion we could be right in time to get it \"associated\" with any given command for which we just got a status back from annex.\n\nBatching -- would only partially resolve the issue since we would need to batch them for execution, until the point when we can't, i.e. next command depends on the output of previous one.  Whenever batching is obvious (e.g. requesting status on presence of files etc) we already can do that by providing multiple files in cmdline to call annex.  \n\nActually for some we should have already filed a wishlist bug report: e.g. in \"annex find\" it can accept multiple files as input, but if a single file among those is not under annex --  it blows, so we have to re-ask one-by-one.  Would it be reasonable to have e.g. an option \"--missing=VALUE\" which would then report VALUE if file is not under annex?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1430423553,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk3OTQ4MTM1","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-97948135"},"message":"Yaroslav Halchenko wrote:\n\n\u003e Actually for some we should have already filed a wishlist bug report: e.g. in\n\u003e \"annex find\" it can accept multiple files as input, but if a single file among\n\u003e those is not under annex -- it blows, so we have to re-ask one-by-one. Would it\n\u003e be reasonable to have e.g. an option \"--missing=VALUE\" which would then report\n\u003e VALUE if file is not under annex?\n\nThis is basically the same problem being discussed in this bug report:\n\nhttp://git-annex.branchable.com/bugs/regression:_behavior_when_files_to_add_do_not_exist/\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1430423555,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk3OTQ4MTQz","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-97948143"},"message":"Could use a socket or extra FD as an extra data channel. It would be an\nunusual interface though and make the client probably need to use a\nnonblocking select loop so rather more work in the client.\n\nOr it could use json output, at least for commands that have json\nsupport. --json mode should disable other extraneous output to stdout.\n\nBut I'd like to investigate adding --batch to individual commands first,\nsince this seems more git-like, and also simpler. It would probably be\nhelpful to talk about the specific commands you need to call a lot.\n\nThings like `git annex lookupkey --batch`, `git-annex readpresentkey --batch`\netc should be able to be spun up and run as long-duration servers, which\nyou could query as needed, not batched up all at once. This is how\ngit-annex uses `git cat-file --batch` etc.\n\nThere's some potential for such a long-running command to either\nbuffer stale data so it doesn't answer with the current state of the\nrepository, or for it to buffer changes and not commit them to disk\nimmediately. For example, a `git annex add --batch` would have the\nlatter problem.\n\nThat is actually an argument for only adding --batch mode to specific\ncommands though, since that would be an opportunity to check thier\nbehavior. A single `git-annex shell` interface would expose any such\nproblems in all commands.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1430724513,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk4NjA5MzA2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-98609306"},"message":"\u003e Or it could use json output, at least for commands that have json\n\u003e support. --json mode should disable other extraneous output to stdout.\n\nIndeed I didn't realize there was a \"--json\" option for \"find\", too. We only use it with \"whereis\" by now. Will change that.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1430934741,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk5NTUxOTM0","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-99551934"},"message":"I've implemented a --batch mode for contentlocation, examinekey, and\nlookupkey. This reads a line, replies with a response (typically on its\nown line, but --format can change the number of lines), and repeats.\n\nIt should be easy to add --batch to other plumbing commands, and less\neasy for non-plumbing commands. I suspect these plumbing query commands\nare where you're seeing overhead of multiple calls though. \n\n1000 calls to git-annex examinekey take 45 seconds; in batch mode that\ndrops to 0.08 seconds. It's calculating a pure function on each key\nwhich is quite fast; the time difference is due to git-annex's startup\noverhead (mostly its need to run git config --list). \n\ncontentlocation has a similar time profile to examinekey; runs about\nhalf as fast since it does need to stat files.\n\n1000 calls to git-annex lookupkey take 73 seconds; batch mode takes \n0.52 seconds. Here it's able to reuse a single git cat-file --batch,\nrather than starting that up 100 times.\n\n## \n\nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1430935163,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDk5NTUzNDI2","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-99553426"},"message":"Sweet! Now we just need to figure out how Rio use it efficiently :-) thanks!\n\nOn May 6, 2015 1:52:21 PM EDT, Joey Hess notifications@github.com wrote:\n\n\u003e I've implemented a --batch mode for contentlocation, examinekey, and\n\u003e lookupkey. This reads a line, replies with a response (typically on its\n\u003e own line, but --format can change the number of lines), and repeats.\n\u003e \n\u003e It should be easy to add --batch to other plumbing commands, and less\n\u003e easy for non-plumbing commands. I suspect these plumbing query commands\n\u003e are where you're seeing overhead of multiple calls though. \n\u003e \n\u003e 1000 calls to git-annex examinekey take 45 seconds; in batch mode that\n\u003e drops to 0.08 seconds. It's calculating a pure function on each key\n\u003e which is quite fast; the time difference is due to git-annex's startup\n\u003e overhead (mostly its need to run git config --list). \n\u003e \n\u003e contentlocation has a similar time profile to examinekey; runs about\n\u003e half as fast since it does need to stat files.\n\u003e \n\u003e 1000 calls to git-annex lookupkey take 73 seconds; batch mode takes \n\u003e 0.52 seconds. Here it's able to reuse a single git cat-file --batch,\n\u003e rather than starting that up 100 times.\n\u003e \n\u003e ## \n\u003e \n\u003e see shy jo\n\u003e \n\u003e ---\n\u003e \n\u003e Reply to this email directly or view it on GitHub:\n\u003e https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-99551934","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1431048052,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDEwMDA2MTQ1Mg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-100061452"},"message":"@bpoldrack I guess we will close this one whenever we start using --batch, reassigning to you ;-)  I guess we could do it \"lazily\": provide an option for those commands \"batch=False\", so if any of those commands requested in batch mode, they initiate a process exchange with corresponding git annex command and later requests go through it instead of the direct \"git annex\" cmdline invocation.  Upon 'del' those should die off and kill corresponding annex as a result","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1515710759,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDM1NzA4NjUxNA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/139#issuecomment-357086514"},"message":"I think we use `--batch` in majority of the cases where it could be used if not in all. will close this one for now","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1515710759,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQyMDc2NjA2NA=="},"status":2}]}